window.__LDNDRC_PAGES = Object.assign(window.__LDNDRC_PAGES || {}, {"nodes-api":"<p>A laptop that wants to become a Host has no credential. The nodes API is built around that fact: it lets strangers knock, but only an operator can open the door.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-states\">The four states</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">register → pending → approved → joined\n                 └─▶ denied</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"register-unauthenticated-by-design\">Register — unauthenticated by design</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl -X POST <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/nodes/register \\\n  -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Content-Type: application/json&#x27;</span> \\\n  -d <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;hostname&quot;:&quot;laptop-a&quot;,&quot;os&quot;:&quot;linux&quot;,&quot;arch&quot;:&quot;amd64&quot;,\n       &quot;cpu&quot;:12,&quot;ram_gb&quot;:32,&quot;gpu&quot;:&quot;NVIDIA RTX&quot;}&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># → {&quot;id&quot;:&quot;&lt;id&gt;&quot;,&quot;status&quot;:&quot;pending&quot;}</span></code></pre></div><p>This endpoint is rate-limited and can require an optional pre-shared <code>JOIN_KEY</code>. It cannot be CSRF-protected, because the caller has no session yet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"approve-operator-only\">Approve — operator only</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl -X POST <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/nodes/&lt;<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">id</span>&gt;/approve -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span>\ncurl -X POST <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/nodes/&lt;<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">id</span>&gt;/deny -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span> -d <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;reason&quot;:&quot;unknown device&quot;}&#x27;</span></code></pre></div><p>Approval is a human decision, and it is the security boundary. Hardware checks tell you whether a machine <em>can</em> be a Host; this tells you whether it <em>may</em> be one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"poll-the-token-mint\">Poll — the token mint</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/nodes/&lt;<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">id</span>&gt;\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># {&quot;status&quot;:&quot;approved&quot;,&quot;k3s_url&quot;:&quot;https://192.168.1.50:6443&quot;,</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#  &quot;k3s_token&quot;:&quot;K10...&quot;,&quot;node_name&quot;:&quot;ldndrc-&lt;id&gt;&quot;}</span></code></pre></div><p>The K3s token is minted on the <strong>first poll after approval</strong>. It is short-lived and single-use from the client&#39;s perspective: the laptop joins, and the token is no longer useful.</p>\n<p>If <code>K3S_JOIN_URL</code> is unset, approved polls answer <code>503</code>. That is a configuration error, not a bug.</p>\n<h2 id=\"joined-confirmed-not-assumed\">Joined — confirmed, not assumed</h2><p>A 5-second in-cluster watcher looks for Ready nodes and labels them <code>role=host</code>. Only then is the node marked <code>joined</code>. This is why approval and readiness are separate states: approving a laptop that never manages to join should be visible, not hidden.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-demo-shortcut\">The demo shortcut</h2><p><code>AUTO_APPROVE=true</code> exists for development. It is never used in a workshop, because it removes the only human check in the onboarding path.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl -s <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$BASE</span>/api/nodes -H <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer <span class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN</span>&quot;</span> | python3 -m json.tool\nkubectl get nodes -L node-role.kubernetes.io/role</code></pre></div>","join-binary":"<p>Passing a binary around on a USB stick does not scale past one workshop. <code>ldndrc-join</code> is the guided installer that replaced it: one download, a hardware verdict, an explanation, and only then a request to join.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-does-in-order\">What it does, in order</h2><ol>\n<li><strong>Detects hardware</strong> — cores, RAM, GPU, disk. No cluster contact yet.</li>\n<li><strong>Shows the verdict</strong> — Host or Guest, with the reason, not just a colour.</li>\n<li><strong>Sniffs for the master</strong> — mDNS <code>_ldndrc-master._tcp</code>, no IP typing.</li>\n<li><strong>Asks before downloading anything</strong> — driver consent is an explicit step (rule D4).</li>\n<li><strong>Registers and polls</strong> — the laptop asks to join; it waits for a human.</li>\n<li><strong>Hands off to the agent</strong> — only after approval does the K3s install happen.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"the-decisions-behind-it\">The decisions behind it</h2><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>D1</td>\n<td>Never install a driver silently</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D2</td>\n<td>Pre-flight is read-only; nothing changes before consent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D3</td>\n<td>Diagnostics upload is separate from network health</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D4</td>\n<td>Driver download requires explicit consent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>These are product rules, not technical ones. They exist because the person running this is a workshop attendee, not an operator with sudo habits.</p>\n<h2 id=\"distribution\">Distribution</h2><p>The binary ships from GitHub Releases, built by <a href=\"#/release-pipeline\" class=\"topic-link\">The release pipeline</a>. Five flavours: Linux, Windows, and macOS on both common architectures, each with a SHA-256 checksum.</p>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">./ldndrc-join            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># full TUI</span>\n./ldndrc-join --plain    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># non-interactive, for scripts and tests</span>\n./ldndrc-join --detect-only</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"current-status\">Current status</h2><p>The TUI spec is a <strong>proposal</strong>. The working path today is still <code>join-cluster.sh</code> plus the captive portal in <a href=\"#/captive-portal\" class=\"topic-link\">The captive onboarding portal</a>. The binary&#39;s engine, dashboard, and plain mode exist and are tested; the richer screens described in the spec are the designed-but-not-shipped part. The map marks this topic <code>partial</code> for exactly that reason.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">./ldndrc-join --plain\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># prints the Host/Guest verdict for this machine, exit 0</span></code></pre></div>","captive-portal":"<p>A laptop that has never joined the cluster cannot download anything from the cluster. That is the chicken-and-egg problem the onboarding portal solves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-setup\">The setup</h2><p>The master hosts a <strong>separate onboarding Wi-Fi</strong>, distinct from the cluster router. A laptop joins it and expects internet. Instead it gets a real page served by the control panel — the OS may even show its &quot;sign in to network&quot; prompt, because the redirect trap makes the network behave like a captive portal.</p>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\"># on the joining laptop — no internet required</span>\ncurl http://&lt;master-ip&gt;:8082/portal\ncurl -O http://&lt;master-ip&gt;:8082/portal/joining-cluster.sh    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Linux/macOS</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Windows:</span>\ncurl -O http://&lt;master-ip&gt;:8082/portal/joining-cluster.ps1</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"the-enrollment-flow\">The enrollment flow</h2><ol>\n<li>The script registers the laptop — idempotent, safe to re-run.</li>\n<li>The instructor sees the request in the console and approves or denies it.</li>\n<li>The script polls until approved, then tries to switch Wi-Fi to the cluster network.</li>\n<li>If the switch fails, it prints manual instructions rather than pretending.</li>\n<li>Approved Hosts are offered the K3s agent install.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"the-download-counter\">The download counter</h2><p>The portal shows a live count of <strong>served</strong> downloads. The semantics are deliberately narrow:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a request that is served counts</li>\n<li>a request that is redirected or blocked does not</li>\n<li>no device identifiers are stored</li>\n<li>the public endpoint exposes only aggregate numbers</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>/api/downloads/stats</code> gives requests plus a unique-device estimate and requires a JWT.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-this-is-and-is-not\">What this is and is not</h2><p>The <code>tools/captive-portal/run.sh</code> harness starts the panel plus the redirect trap and prints the three root steps: hotspot, dnsmasq, nftables. It is a <strong>workshop rig</strong>, not gateway enforcement. A laptop can still reach the cluster router if it knows the SSID.</p>\n<p>Gateway enforcement is a designed follow-up, not shipped. The map reflects that in the topic&#39;s status.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">./tools/captive-portal/run.sh status\n./test-captive-trap.sh\ncurl -s http://&lt;master-ip&gt;:8082/api/downloads/count</code></pre></div>","release-pipeline":"<p>Tagging a version triggers GitHub Actions to build the join binary for every laptop flavour, test it, checksum it, and publish a release. No USB sticks, no <code>scp</code>, no &quot;which build do you have?&quot;.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-produces\">What it produces</h2><ul>\n<li>Linux, Windows, and macOS builds</li>\n<li>both common architectures per platform</li>\n<li>a SHA-256 checksum beside each binary</li>\n<li>a release page with copy-paste install lines</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"two-deliberate-choices\">Two deliberate choices</h2><p><strong>No GoReleaser.</strong> Plain workshop tools, fewer moving parts, zero cost. The workflow file is readable by anyone who might need to fix it.</p>\n<p><strong>Tests gate the release.</strong> A broken build never ships, which is the only reason publishing is safe to do from a tag.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-lesson-it-recorded\">The lesson it recorded</h2><p>Renaming a downloaded binary breaks its checksum verification. The spec now says: keep the release filename. That bug was caught by the project&#39;s own verification steps, not by a user — which is the point of having them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">gh release list\ngh release view &lt;tag&gt; --json assets</code></pre></div>","joiner-tui":"<p>The TUI spec describes what a first-time attendee sees, screen by screen. It is the human side of <a href=\"#/join-binary\" class=\"topic-link\">The join binary</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"screen-1-pre-flight-read-only\">Screen 1 — pre-flight, read-only</h2><p>A matrix of checks: cores, RAM, GPU, disk, network, OS. Nothing is installed, nothing is uploaded. The user can read the verdict before agreeing to anything.</p>\n<h2 id=\"screen-2-the-verdict\">Screen 2 — the verdict</h2><p>Host or Guest, with the failing row highlighted and a plain-language reason. &quot;16 GB required, this laptop has 15&quot; is a better message than a red cross.</p>\n<h2 id=\"screens-3-consent-and-handoff\">Screens 3+ — consent and handoff</h2><p>Driver download consent (D4), diagnostics upload consent (D3), registration, waiting for approval, agent install. Each step is skippable with a clear consequence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-rules-that-constrain-the-design\">The rules that constrain the design</h2><ul>\n<li>Never install without consent.</li>\n<li>Never upload diagnostics without consent.</li>\n<li>Never claim a step succeeded without checking.</li>\n<li>Always offer a manual path when automation fails.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"status\">Status</h2><p>The screens are <strong>designed, not shipped</strong>. The engine and <code>--plain</code> mode exist; the richer TUI is the proposal. The map marks the topic accordingly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">./ldndrc-join --detect-only</code></pre></div>"});
